# The Perimeter

### Paper I was the root. This is everything the root touches — and who is standing there when it does.

**Anonymous security research — November 2026**
**Companion to "One Root: The Session Identity Architecture." This paper covers disjoint ground: the first paper examined what a Session account *is*. This one examines where a Session client *goes* — every external system it contacts, what each contact identifies, and how those contacts join together. Signed by the same key.**

- ---

## Abstract

Session's public story is a network with no center: messages move through a decentralized
service-node swarm over onion routing, and no operator is positioned to observe the whole.
The swarm is real. It is also only the middle of the journey. A Session client, in ordinary
default use, also contacts Google's Firebase infrastructure, Signal's content proxy, the
foundation's bootstrap seed servers, the foundation's call relays, and legacy storage
namespaces that accept unauthenticated reads and writes. Each of those contacts is
identified, most are persistent, and several of them join to each other — including one
join that links a user's successive accounts on the same device, in the operator's own
database, whether the user changed identities or not.

Nothing in this paper concerns message cryptography; that was Paper I. This paper is a
map of the perimeter: the entrances, exits, and auxiliaries around the onion layer, each
verified from the shipped code and the operator's own server repositories, at the same
pins and releases as Paper I (Android 1.33.7, iOS 2.15.5, source HEADs re-fetched
November 3, 2026).

## 1. Bootstrap: the decentralized network has three front doors, and they see your real IP

A fresh Session client does not know any service nodes. It learns them from one of three
hard-coded foundation servers — `seed1.getsession.org`, `seed2.getsession.org`,
`seed3.getsession.org`. The fetch is a **direct HTTPS POST** (`get_n_service_nodes`),
executed on a plain HTTP stack, *not* over the onion layer (Android,
`SnodeDirectory.kt`: `fetchSnodePoolFromSeedWithFallback`, iterating the three seeds
until one answers; if all fail, a snapshot bundled in the app assets is used). Desktop
performs the same direct fetch (`SeedNodeAPI.ts`, via the aptly named
`insecureNodeFetch`; the code logs the request as "plaintext for getSnodesFromSeedUrl,"
meaning non-onion — TLS applies to https URLs) and sends the User-Agent **"WhatsApp"** —
a fingerprint in itself, for any observer who knows the pattern.

The bounds matter and are stated: TLS hides the *content* of the bootstrap exchange.
What it cannot hide:

1. The seed servers are positioned to log the **real IP of every bootstrapping client**.
   Bootstrap happens at fresh installs, new devices, and after long absences — the
   new-device and new-network moments, precisely when a user is most identifiable.
   The FAQ's claim that it is "impossible for Session to track users' IP addresses
   because the app uses onion requests" does not describe bootstrap, because bootstrap
   is not an onion request.
2. The entire node list a fresh client trusts **descends from three named foundation
   hosts**. Control or compulsion of the seeds is control over a new user's whole view
   of the "decentralized" network.
3. To any network observer, a connection to `seed*.getsession.org` is an unambiguous
   signal: *Session starts here.*

Routine refreshes, once a client is inside, query pool members over onion requests —
the good path, and it is noted as such. The exposure is concentrated exactly where a
user has the least protection: the door.

## 2. Calls leave the architecture entirely

Voice and video calls do not use the onion network at all. They are WebRTC, with a
foundation TURN fleet: `turn:{freyr, angus, hereford, holstein, brahman}.getsession.org`,
two chosen at random per call (Android `PeerConnectionWrapper.kt`; desktop
`CallManager.ts` carries the identical fleet; iOS the same architecture).

The fleet is authenticated with **one static credential pair, shared by every install
since November 2021**, printed in every shipped client: username `session202111`,
password `053c268164bc7bd7`. A relay network whose password is public cannot
distinguish its users at the authentication layer at all; anyone who extracts the
credential from the app — a five-minute job — can consume the fleet.

Worse for the callers than for the fleet: the relay-only ICE mode exists in the code
and is **off by default**. Default calls attempt direct peer paths first, so **your
call partner learns your real IP address** — a fact the application itself warns about
in its own UI. When TURN carries or falls back a call, the foundation's relay
necessarily sees **both endpoints' IPs together with timing, duration, and volume**.

The media itself is DTLS-SRTP encrypted; the exposure is pairing and IP metadata, not
content — and it is total for that metadata class. Two public claims do not describe
this path: the FAQ's "impossible for Session to track users' IP addresses," and the
App Store's "Device IP addresses are never exposed to the person you're talking to or
the servers holding your data." Calls are not onion requests. They are WebRTC flows
through named foundation hosts.

## 3. Google holds an identity for your install

The default Android build (Play flavor; the F-Droid build compiles the same messaging
stack) contains Firebase — re-admitted in June 2020, four months after a celebrated
purge of Google dependencies, and formalized in the 2023 flavor split. The website
build contains none, which establishes that the Firebase-free configuration exists;
it is not the build most users run.

The shipped APK contains the Firebase Installations endpoint
(`firebaseinstallations.googleapis.com/v1/...`). That service's entire function is to
mint and maintain a **persistent per-install identifier** — the Firebase Installation
ID — on Google's servers, for the life of the install.

The client contains genuine hardening, and it is recorded here because the paper's
standard is the whole record: the manifest deactivates Firebase Analytics collection,
Crashlytics collection, SSAID and advertising-ID collection, and disables messaging
auto-init. The gap is that none of that prevents registration. Fast-mode notifications
bind a token fetcher (`FirebaseTokenFetcher`, `src/firebaseCommon`) whose
initialization calls `FirebaseMessaging.getInstance().token` whenever no token is
held. **Obtaining an FCM token forces the SDK to register the installation with
Firebase Installations first.** Auto-init being off does not help; the explicit token
call performs the initialization on demand. Registration events — install identity,
IP, timing, app identity — are visible to Google.

Bound, precisely: Firebase Installations exposes **no message content and no contacts**
to Google. It is an identity layer, not a content layer. Its significance is what it
joins to — §4.

On devices without Google services the FCM path fails and the app falls back to
slow-mode polling; no registration occurs. The exposure described in §§3–4 is the
default path: Play build, fast mode, the configuration onboarding presents.

## 4. New account, same device: the operator can tell

A natural question follows from Paper I: if an account's identity is permanent and
unrotatable, can a user at least start over — delete the account, create a fresh
Session ID — and be unlinked? Within the protocol, yes: a new seed is a new identity,
and this audit found **no protocol-level device key** (the per-account notification
key is generated fresh with each account; the one carry-over in the code is the
legacy-to-current migration of the *same* account, which is not a cross-account link).

At the deployed layer, no. The link is the push token:

- - Push tokens (FCM on Android, APNs on iOS) are issued **per app installation, not
  per account**.
- - Fast-mode registration hands the foundation's push server a binding of
  **{Session ID, push token, device type, namespaces, group keys}** — the client's
  registration message, verified in `PushRegisterApi.kt`, and stored server-side in a
  PostgreSQL subscriptions table **unique per (pubkey, service, token)**.
- - Create a new account on the same phone, and the new identity registers for fast
  mode with **the same token**. The operator's own subscription table now holds —
  across time, in one place — two Session IDs bound to one installation token.
  That is a same-device join, readable by the operator, requiring no inference and
  no third party.
- - On Google's side, the same installation retains the same Firebase Installation ID
  (§3), and the audit's earlier formulation stands verbatim: *the token is the join
  key between Google's install record and the foundation's account/group map.*

The bounds: uninstalling and reinstalling rotates both the token and the FID — the
join is per *installation*, and a genuinely fresh install breaks it. Slow mode
creates no registration and no join. But the user who does the intuitive thing —
new account, same phone, same app — has changed their identity everywhere except
the one database operated by the foundation itself.

## 5. Six years of GIFs, proxied by Signal

The Android client's build configuration still sets `CONTENT_PROXY_HOST` to Signal's
content proxy (`app/build.gradle.kts:155-156`, port 443). The inherited Signal Giphy
plumbing survives verbatim: `ContentProxySelector` whitelists only the Giphy domain
and throws otherwise; `GiphyGifLoader` calls the Giphy API with the lineage API key.
Every GIF search and trending fetch from Session Android is CONNECT-tunneled through
**Signal's proxy infrastructure**.

Stated with bounds: TLS inside the tunnel means the proxy does not see search terms.
It sees what a proxy always sees — **the Session user's IP address, fetching Giphy
content, with timing**. Giphy never sees the user; Signal sees Session's users,
indefinitely, because in roughly six years nobody repointed the host. It is the only
Signal-domain reference left in the source or configuration. The application ships a
`giphyWarning` dialog — the exposure is known to the project. The mitigation simply
still points at the original vendor, in an application whose founding story is
independence from that vendor.

## 6. A legacy namespace that answers to no one

The storage server still operates namespace **-10**, "LegacyClosed," at current HEAD
(`oxenss/common/namespace.h`). Its own header comment describes it, and the comment
is quoted here because no paraphrase improves it:

> "allows unauthenticated store *and* retrieval. Deprecated and will be removed from
> a future version once no longer used."

Deprecated in the comment; live in the code. Any party can store messages into, and
retrieve messages from, the legacy closed-group namespace **without authentication**.
The namespace also satisfies the public-inbox divisibility rule, widening its
reachability. However small the remaining legacy traffic is, a network that markets
itself on having no unauthenticated surfaces operates one, years into its deprecation,
for the most sensitive namespace class it has — closed groups.

## 7. The first push server kept no secrets from the providers

The current push design (operator custody of wrapped payloads) is Paper I's subject.
The 2020–2023 server — the Python v1 that served the product's first years — was
worse, and its history is recorded in the project's own repository
(`pushNotificationHandler.py`, final revisions March 2022):

- - **No wrap at all.** The swarm message bytes were placed directly into provider
  payloads: iOS received a custom APNs payload containing `{'ENCRYPTED_DATA':
  message['data'], 'remote': 1}`; Android an FCM message with
  `data={'ENCRYPTED_DATA': message['data']}`. The sealed ciphertexts of every DM
  and group message for every fast-mode user of that era transited Apple and Google
  in directly usable form.
- - **The server resolved closed-group membership itself.** Recipient fan-out for
  closed groups was computed server-side (`get_closed_group(recipient).members`) —
  the old server held **group membership maps**, not merely device tokens.

Why a retired server belongs in a current paper: copies do not retire when servers
do. A ciphertext copied into a provider archive, a log, or a legal-process return in
2021 remains exactly as decryptable in 2026 — because, per the property established
in Paper I, none of those ciphertexts' keys can ever change. The swarm's ~14-day
storage TTL, the number the decentralization story quotes, never applied to the
copies the perimeter made.

## 8. "Whispers" are a flag, not a lock

Community servers (SOGS) offer "whisper" posts — presented to users as messages
visible only to the author and moderators. In the server implementation
(`session-pysogs`, `routes/messages.py` and schema), a whisper is a **visibility flag
enforced by the API**. The post is stored in the same messages table, in the same
form as every public post — the submitted body plus the author's signature — and
moderators can be granted read access by design.

Nothing about a whisper is encrypted, sealed, or cryptographically restricted. Its
privacy is a policy decision executed by the server operator's software, revocable
by anyone with the database. Users whispering in a community are relying on a
boolean.

## 9. The analytics chapter: what the pre-release knew

Session descends, in its own repository history, from Signal's TextSecure — the
history begins December 20, 2011 with Moxie Marlinspike's "Initial Project Import"
commit, and the Loki fork takes over in April–June 2019. What the fork team added
before Session was published is the chapter its privacy story does not mention:

- - **September 4–5, 2019: Mixpanel analytics set up and merged** (`e346a45d99f`,
  PR #20). **September 6, 2019: Crashlytics set up** (`83742a0fc20`).
  **October 7, 2019: "Match iOS analytics"** — the iOS line had it too.
- - The tracked events (commit `6341ca01012`, "Add basic events") included
  "Conversation Opened," "Loki Public Chat Opened," "RSS Feed Opened," "QR Code
  Scanned," "New Conversation Started" — and, in a privacy messenger, **"Seed
  Created"** and **"Seed Restored"**: behavioral events, sent to Mixpanel's
  third-party servers, throughout the product's pre-release life.
- - **The removal was a launch-eve scrub, not a design principle.** Mixpanel was
  removed January 31, 2020 (`67cc92d9835`); Crashlytics on February 5, 2020
  (`c50b599ea5`) — **one day before the Session 1.0.0 tag (February 6, 2020)**.

The honest present tense, verified in the current tree and the shipped 1.33.7 APK:
the launch build was clean, and **no vendor analytics pipeline persists today** —
no analytics SDKs or call sites in the current Android source, Firebase Analytics
explicitly excluded in gradle, desktop clean. Everyone who ran the pre-release
builds between September 2019 and January 2020 was analytics-tracked; the public
product launched without the trackers, and the record of how close to launch they
survived is in the project's own history for anyone who reads it. The chapter is
included not because it is current exposure, but because it is the founding
precedent for the pattern this paper documents: the perimeter is where the public
story and the shipped reality have always diverged first.

## 10. The map, assembled

Join the contacts, and the perimeter of a default Session installation is:

- - **Foundation seed servers** — your real IP, at bootstrap, at the moments you are
  newest to the network (§1).
- - **Foundation TURN relays** — your IP paired with your call partner's, with call
  timing and duration; your call partner — your IP directly, by default (§2).
- - **Google** — a persistent installation identity, registration events, and (via the
  token the foundation also holds) per-message wake-up timing (§§3–4).
- - **Apple / Huawei** — the analogous token-side position on their platforms.
- - **The foundation's push server** — the standing join of Session ID ↔ device token
  ↔ namespaces ↔ groups, including the join between your successive accounts on one
  device (§4).
- - **Signal** — your IP, fetching GIFs, with timing, for six years and counting (§5).
- - **Anyone at all** — unauthenticated storage and retrieval in the legacy closed-group
  namespace (§6), and, for the v1 era's retained copies, sealed ciphertexts whose
  keys can never rotate (§7).

The onion layer protects message transport between service nodes. It was never the
whole system. Every entrance, exit, auxiliary, and leftover around it is operated by
the foundation, by a platform incumbent, or — in the case of §6 — by nobody, for
anybody.

## 11. What this paper does not claim

- - Firebase Installations exposes no content or contacts to Google; it is an identity
  and registration layer (§3).
- - Signal's proxy cannot read GIF search terms; it observes IP, destination, and
  timing (§5).
- - TURN media is encrypted; the call findings are metadata findings (§2).
- - Bootstrap content is TLS-protected; the findings are positioning and trust-root
  findings (§1).
- - The v1 push server is retired; §7 is history with present-tense consequences for
  retained copies, not a description of the current server (Paper I covers that).
- - No vendor analytics pipeline exists in the current product; §9 says so in terms.
- - The protocol contains no device key; the cross-account device join lives at the
  deployed push layer, and a fresh reinstall breaks it (§4).
- - Slow mode avoids the push server entirely, at real usability cost; it is the
  existing escape hatch for §§3–4 and part of §10, and almost nobody is told to use it.

## 12. What a fix requires

In outline, per system: onion-routed or snapshot-only bootstrap that never exposes a
bootstrapping IP to a foundation log (§1); relay-only calls by default, per-user TURN
credentials, and claims corrected to describe the call path (§2); a push design that
does not require a persistent third-party installation identity, or honest disclosure
of the one it uses (§3); token hygiene that treats account changes as identity events
— and a subscription store that cannot join successive accounts by construction (§4);
a content proxy operated by the project, or none (§5); the legacy namespace actually
removed (§6); public retention accounting for perimeter copies (§7); whispers
implemented cryptographically or renamed to what they are (§8).

None of these requires new mathematics. All of them require the perimeter to be
treated as part of the product's privacy surface — which, for its users, it already is.

## Disclosure

The vendor was notified on November 3, 2026, in the correspondence accompanying
Paper I; the co-founder who replied directed us to the current Session Technology
Foundation team, and notification follows that redirect. The standing offer applies
to this paper identically: any vendor response will be appended, unedited, with its
receipt date, and any specific error identified will be corrected in this document,
publicly, with the correction credited.

